Title:
I’m watching you! How to spy Windows users via MS UIA

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explains how to abuse Microsoft UI Automation (MS UIA) as a user-spying mechanism by subscribing to UIA events and reading UI element properties across applications. It demonstrates building a UIA client in C++ on top of COM to monitor text entry, selection changes, window opens, and property changes, effectively enabling a “GUI keylogger” without traditional keyboard hooks. The author covers the UIA automation/control tree, element discovery, and event handler registration (automation events and property-changed handlers), then routes captured events by originating process to implement app-specific harvesting logic. A companion PoC, “Spyndicapped,” shows targeted collection from apps like Firefox (e.g., Slack/WhatsApp Web) and KeePass by automating UI interactions (e.g., invoking “Copy Password”). It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters and malware developers exploring stealthy user-data collection via legitimate accessibility APIs, and it’s interesting because UIA-based telemetry can bypass simplistic EDR detections focused on low-level input hooks.

Technical Focus:
- MS UI Automation (UIA) architecture: elements, properties, patterns, events
- COM-based UIA client development (CoCreateInstance, IUIAutomation*)
- Automation/Control Tree traversal (IUIAutomationTreeWalker, TreeScope_Subtree)
- Event subscription and handling (IUIAutomationEventHandler, property-changed handlers)
- Process attribution from UIA elements (PID/module name mapping)
- UI-driven data theft/automation via UIA patterns (Invoke/Value/Selection)

Use Cases:
- Build a UIA-based “GUI keylogger” capturing text/value changes without keyboard hooks
- Monitor sensitive app workflows (browsers, chat apps, password managers) via UI events
- Automate UI interactions for credential extraction (e.g., triggering “Copy Password” in KeePass)
- Red-team tradecraft research into living-off-the-land style collection using accessibility APIs
- Defensive testing: validate detections/controls around UIA abuse and suspicious UI automation

Keywords:
MS UIA, UI Automation, Accessibility, COM, CoCreateInstance, IUIAutomation, IUIAutomationElement, IUIAutomationEventHandler, IUIAutomationPropertyChangedEventHandler, AddAutomationEventHandler, AddPropertyChangedEventHandler, Automation Tree, Control Tree, TreeWalker, TreeScope_Subtree, UIA_TextChangedEventId, UIA_Invoke_InvokedEventId, inspect.exe, accevent.exe, KeePass, Slack, WhatsApp Web, GUI automation, credential theft